Z={
1:"Madhusūdana says Arjuna still thinks the two paths cannot be combined. Teachers often meet this in students: a wish for a single rule, when the truth needs balance.",
2:"Commentators note niḥśreyasa-karau, “leading to the highest good”: both paths reach freedom. Madhusūdana says karma yoga is better for one not yet ready for full renunciation. Some modern teachers compare it to choosing between climbing a mountain on foot or by a steeper direct route: both reach the top, but most people need the gentler path.",
3:"Commentators note sukham, “easily”: the inner renunciant is freed without struggle. Madhusūdana says such a person is a renunciant even while working. Many householder saints, like Kabīr the weaver and Tukārām the grocer, lived this verse.",
4:"Commentators note ekam apy āsthitaḥ samyak, “established properly even in one”: thoroughness in one path is enough. Madhusūdana says the fruit of both is liberation. Teachers often advise students to go deep in one practice rather than shallow in many.",
5:"Commentators note sthānam, “state” or “place”: the goal reached by both. Madhusūdana says it is liberation. The verse is quoted to end quarrels between followers of different paths. The image of many rivers reaching one ocean is often used with it.",
6:"Commentators note na cireṇa, “before long”: the yogi joined in karma yoga soon reaches Brahman. Madhusūdana says karma yoga purifies, and the pure mind quickly grasps the truth. The verse encourages those who feel their daily work is not spiritual enough.",
7:"Commentators note jitendriya, “senses conquered”: self-mastery comes before the universal vision. Madhusūdana says one who sees all beings as himself cannot act selfishly. Many find that genuine empathy changes behaviour more than rules do.",
8:"Commentators note tattva-vit, “knower of the truth”: the vision comes from knowledge, not pretending. Madhusūdana says the senses act, but the Self watches. Some meditation methods ask practitioners to note each action as it happens: walking, lifting, eating. This builds the witnessing awareness described here.",
9:"Commentators note that the list includes involuntary acts, breathing and blinking, along with voluntary ones. Madhusūdana says the knower sees no difference: all are acts of nature. This removes pride in voluntary acts and anxiety about involuntary ones.",
10:"Commentators note brahmaṇy ādhāya, “placing in Brahman”: actions are offered to the Supreme. Madhusūdana says the offering removes attachment, and without attachment sin does not cling. The lotus became a symbol of purity across India; it is the national flower of India today.",
11:"Commentators note that body, mind, intellect and senses are all listed as instruments of karma yoga. Madhusūdana says even the senses alone, without the sense of doership, can do pure work. Many find that physical work, done with care, calms the mind.",
12:"Commentators note naiṣṭhikī, “lasting”: the peace of the karma yogi is not a passing mood. Madhusūdana says it comes from purity of mind. The contrast with the one bound by desire is sharp: same work, different heart, different result.",
13:"Commentators note vaśī, “self-controlled”: the dweller in the city of nine gates is its master. Madhusūdana says he neither acts nor causes action, in the sense of claiming doership. Some teachers suggest a simple practice: before sleep, mentally close the nine gates and rest in the centre.",
14:"Commentators note svabhāva, one’s own nature, as the force that drives action. Madhusūdana says this nature is ignorance, māyā, in Advaita terms. Practical teachers note that nature can be reshaped slowly by habit, company and practice; the verse invites that work rather than blaming fate.",
15:"Commentators note jantavaḥ, “creatures”: all beings are deluded by ignorance, not only humans. Madhusūdana says the Lord does not take on anyone’s merit or sin; each reaps his own. This removes the idea of a God who can be bribed or blamed.",
16:"Commentators note tat param, “that Supreme”: knowledge reveals the highest. Madhusūdana says ignorance is like darkness and knowledge like sunrise. The image of the sun rising over darkness is used in many Indian prayers at dawn.",
17:"Commentators note jñāna-nirdhūta-kalmaṣāḥ, “sins shaken off by knowledge”: knowledge cleanses. Madhusūdana says the fourfold absorption, in understanding, self, ground and goal, is complete dedication. Such people do not return to birth.",
18:"Commentators note that the verse says the wise see sama, the same, not that they treat everyone identically. Madhusūdana says the wise respond to each according to need, while seeing the same Self in all. A doctor treats each patient differently, but cares for all equally.",
19:"Commentators note nirdoṣam, “flawless”: Brahman is free of all defect and equal in all. Madhusūdana says one who sees evenly shares this flawlessness. The verse gives a practical test of spiritual progress: am I growing more even-minded?",
20:"Commentators note sthira-buddhiḥ, “steady understanding”, and asammūḍhaḥ, “undeluded”: steadiness comes from clarity. Madhusūdana says the knower of Brahman is not shaken because he knows both pleasant and unpleasant pass. Many people who face illness or loss with calm say that steadiness came from understanding, not from forcing feelings away.",
21:"Commentators note bāhya-sparśa, “outer contacts”: the joy described does not depend on the senses. Madhusūdana says one who finds joy within is not drawn by outer pleasures. Many meditators describe a quiet joy that arises in stillness, needing nothing.",
22:"Commentators note budhaḥ, “the wise”: the wise do not delight in pleasures that end. Madhusūdana says they see the pain hidden in them. This is not gloom but clear sight. One can enjoy a sunset while knowing it will fade.",
23:"Commentators note sukhī, “happy”: the one who can bear the surge of desire and anger is truly happy. Madhusūdana says happiness is not having all one wants, but being free of the compulsion to want. Many find this verse a practical guide in moments of strong emotion.",
24:"Commentators note antar-ārāma, “whose garden is within”: an image of an inner pleasure garden. Madhusūdana says such a person has no need of outer gardens. Ramana Maharshi, who lived for decades in the same small place, is often given as an example.",
25:"Commentators note chinna-dvaidhāḥ, “doubts cut”: clarity is part of freedom. Madhusūdana says the sages who rejoice in all beings’ welfare reach peace. The verse links inner freedom with outer service.",
26:"Commentators note yatīnām, “of ascetics”: those who strive. Madhusūdana says peace surrounds them in life and after death. The verse closes the description of the free person before the brief teaching on meditation.",
27:"Commentators note that the gaze between the eyebrows prevents both sleep and distraction. Madhusūdana says the breath, made even, calms the mind. Modern breathing practices often aim at the same balance of in-breath and out-breath.",
28:"Commentators note mokṣa-parāyaṇa, “intent on freedom”: the meditator has one aim. Madhusūdana says such a one is free even before death. The verse prepares chapter 6’s full teaching on meditation.",
29:"Commentators note that this is the first verse in the Gītā where Kṛṣṇa calls himself the friend of all beings. Madhusūdana says knowing the Lord as friend removes fear and brings peace. Many devotees recite this verse as a daily reminder that they are never alone.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
